      <h2>Why I Decided to Launch It</h2>

      <p>
        Research Wheel started from a relatively simple problem:
        researching something on the web often means constantly moving
        between different tools.
      </p>

      <p>
        You find an interesting piece of information, open another tab
        to search for the source, switch to a citation generator, copy
        something into your notes, and eventually try to remember where
        everything came from.
      </p>

      <p>
        Research Wheel is my attempt to put those actions closer to the
        information you're already looking at.
      </p>

      <h2>What Research Wheel Does</h2>

      <p>
        Research Wheel is a browser extension built around a radial
        research interface. Instead of constantly switching tabs,
        actions can be triggered directly from the page you're
        researching.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can highlight information and quickly access tools for
        searching academic sources, saving quotes, generating citations,
        translating text, copying sources, and organizing findings in a
        persistent research workspace.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project is also designed to be <strong>local-first</strong>.
        It is free and open source, with no tracking or analytics.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'm Hoping to Learn</h2>

      <p>
        The most valuable part of the launch isn't simply getting people
        to see the project. I want to find out whether the problem
        Research Wheel is solving is actually a problem other people
        experience.
      </p>

      <p>
        In particular, I'm interested in hearing from people who regularly
        research through their browser.
      </p>

      <p>
        What part of your research workflow is still annoying?
        Which tools do you constantly switch between?
        What would make browser-based research substantially easier?
      </p>

      <p>
        Those answers are much more useful to me than simply knowing how
        many people downloaded the extension.
      </p>

